-- ===== Methadone CYP2B6/CYP3A4 wording: q_methadone_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p>Methadone is a synthetic opioid used in pain management and opioid substitution therapy. It has unique pharmacological properties that distinguish it from other opioids, including NMDA receptor antagonism and serotonin reuptake inhibition. Its complex pharmacokinetics make dose titration hazardous.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Methadone has a highly variable and unpredictable half-life ranging from 8–120 hours, most commonly quoted as 24–36 hours. This wide variability reflects inter-individual differences in CYP2B6 and CYP3A4 metabolism (with a minor CYP2D6 contribution) and makes accumulation a significant clinical risk.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Methadone prolongs the QTc interval, predisposing to torsades de pointes, particularly at higher doses and in patients with hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, or concurrent use of other QTc-prolonging drugs. A baseline ECG is recommended before initiation.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Methadone is not a pure mu-agonist. It also acts as an NMDA receptor antagonist — contributing to efficacy in neuropathic pain and reducing opioid tolerance — and inhibits reuptake of serotonin and noradrenaline.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Methadone is highly lipophilic and accumulates in fat, muscle and other tissues. With repeated dosing, tissue stores build up and then continue to release drug into plasma — this is why late accumulation and respiratory depression can occur days after what appeared to be a stable dose.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Unlike morphine, whose active metabolite morphine-6-glucuronide accumulates in renal failure, methadone is primarily hepatically metabolised (via CYP2B6 and CYP3A4) and does not require dose reduction in renal failure. This makes it a useful option in patients with chronic kidney disease requiring strong opioids.</p>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_methadone_001';

-- ===== Methadone CYP2B6/CYP3A4 wording: q_mtf_opioid_qtc_prolongation_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p>QTc prolongation is a clinically important consideration in opioid prescribing, as it increases the risk of torsades de pointes and sudden cardiac death; the risk varies markedly between opioids depending on their effect on cardiac potassium channels (hERG channel blockade), and this variability is a well-recognised exam theme.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> — Hydromorphone is not significantly associated with clinically relevant QTc prolongation and is considered one of the safer opioid choices where QTc prolongation is a concern.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Tapentadol has not been shown to cause clinically significant QTc prolongation at therapeutic doses, unlike some other opioids.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Pethidine is not classically associated with QTc prolongation as its primary cardiac concern; its more notable risks relate to its metabolite norpethidine (neuroexcitatory, seizure risk in accumulation) and serotonergic interactions (serotonin syndrome risk with MAOIs/SSRIs), rather than QTc effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Methadone is the opioid most strongly and consistently associated with dose-dependent QTc prolongation, via direct blockade of the cardiac hERG potassium channel. This is a well-established risk requiring baseline and periodic ECG monitoring, particularly at higher doses, with other risk factors (electrolyte disturbance, other QT-prolonging drugs), or with rapid dose escalation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Alfentanil is not significantly associated with QTc prolongation and is generally considered cardiac-safe in this respect, consistent with other fentanyl-derivative opioids.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Opioid Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Hydromorphone</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Semi-synthetic mu-opioid receptor agonist; ~5–7x potency of oral morphine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic glucuronidation (UGT) → hydromorphone-3-glucuronide (H3G, inactive analgesically, neuroexcitatory) — no significant CYP450 involvement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>No significant QTc effect</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Useful alternative to morphine in mild-moderate renal impairment (though H3G still accumulates, requiring caution); available in high-concentration formulations useful in palliative care for small-volume dosing</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Tapentadol</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Dual-mechanism analgesic — mu-opioid receptor agonist and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (NRI); minimal serotonergic activity, distinguishing it from tramadol</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily glucuronidation (UGT) — minimal CYP450 involvement, few active metabolites</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>No significant QTc effect</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Lower incidence of nausea/vomiting and constipation than classical opioids at equianalgesic doses, attributed to lower mu-receptor occupancy for equivalent analgesia; lower serotonin syndrome risk than tramadol due to minimal serotonergic activity; used in both acute nociceptive and neuropathic pain</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Pethidine (Meperidine)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Synthetic mu-opioid receptor agonist with additional local anaesthetic-like (sodium channel blocking) and anticholinergic/antimuscarinic properties</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via N-demethylation → norpethidine, an active, neuroexcitatory metabolite (lowers seizure threshold, causes tremor/myoclonus/agitation) that accumulates with repeated dosing or renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal (norpethidine notably renally cleared and accumulates in renal impairment)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>Not classically associated with QTc prolongation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Significant serotonergic activity — contraindicated with MAOIs (risk of severe serotonin syndrome/hyperpyrexic reactions); historically used in labour analgesia, now largely superseded due to norpethidine toxicity risk and neonatal effects</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Methadone</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Synthetic mu-opioid receptor agonist and NMDA receptor antagonist; also inhibits monoamine (serotonin/noradrenaline) reuptake</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP2B6 and CYP3A4 (minor CYP2D6 contribution) → inactive metabolites; highly variable interpatient pharmacokinetics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Faecal/biliary predominant (advantageous in renal impairment) with a minor renal component</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Long and highly variable half-life (8–60+ hours), poor correlation between plasma level and effect, risk of delayed accumulation/toxicity with dose titration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td><strong>Most significant opioid cause of dose-dependent QTc prolongation</strong> via hERG potassium channel blockade — requires baseline/periodic ECG monitoring, particularly at higher doses or with other risk factors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>NMDA antagonism provides additional utility in neuropathic pain and opioid tolerance/hyperalgesia; complex drug interactions via CYP2B6 and CYP3A4 (many drugs alter methadone levels)</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Alfentanil</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Highly selective mu-opioid receptor agonist; synthetic fentanyl analogue</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolism</strong></td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4 → inactive metabolites</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal (inactive metabolites — no clinically significant accumulation in renal impairment)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></td><td>Rapid onset, short duration of action due to small volume of distribution and rapid redistribution/clearance — shortest context-sensitive half-time of the fentanyl-family opioids, making it useful for short procedures</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cardiac</strong></td><td>No significant QTc effect</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notable features</strong></td><td>Primarily used intraoperatively/for short procedures rather than chronic pain management; potency roughly 1/4–1/10 that of fentanyl but faster onset/offset</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_mtf_opioid_qtc_prolongation_001';

-- ===== Methadone CYP2B6/CYP3A4 wording: q_mtf_opioid_active_metabolites_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Whether an opioid has a clinically significant active metabolite matters most in renal impairment, where renally-cleared metabolites accumulate and cause toxicity. Morphine, pethidine and oxycodone are the classic offenders; fentanyl, alfentanil and methadone are not.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Methadone is metabolised hepatically (via CYP2B6 and CYP3A4, with a minor CYP2D6 contribution) to inactive metabolites (principally EDDP). The absence of an active, renally-cleared metabolite is one reason methadone is relatively favourable in renal impairment, although accumulation of the parent drug and QT prolongation still require caution.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Pethidine undergoes N-demethylation to norpethidine, an active, neuroexcitatory metabolite that lowers the seizure threshold and causes tremor, myoclonus and agitation. Norpethidine accumulates with repeated dosing and in renal impairment, and is the main reason pethidine is avoided for prolonged or repeated use.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Alfentanil is metabolised hepatically via CYP3A4 to inactive metabolites (noralfentanil), with no clinically significant active metabolite accumulation. With fentanyl, it is one of the preferred opioids in renal impairment.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Oxycodone is metabolised via CYP3A4 to noroxycodone (weakly active) and via CYP2D6 to oxymorphone (active, and more potent than the parent). Both the parent drug and its metabolites are renally excreted and accumulate in renal impairment.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Morphine undergoes hepatic glucuronidation to morphine-6-glucuronide (an active analgesic and respiratory depressant) and morphine-3-glucuronide (not analgesic but neuroexcitatory). Both are renally excreted and accumulate substantially in renal impairment, making morphine one of the least suitable opioids in this setting.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Opioid Active Metabolites</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">An active metabolite is one that retains pharmacological activity &mdash; analgesic, respiratory-depressant or neuroexcitatory. The clinical significance depends on the metabolite&rsquo;s potency and its route of clearance: renally-cleared active metabolites accumulate when renal function is poor.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Opioids with clinically significant active metabolites</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Opioid</th><th>Active metabolite(s)</th><th>Clinical consequence</th></tr><tr><td>Morphine</td><td>Morphine-6-glucuronide (active analgesic, respiratory depressant); morphine-3-glucuronide (neuroexcitatory, non-analgesic)</td><td>Accumulate in renal impairment &mdash; sedation, respiratory depression, myoclonus</td></tr><tr><td>Pethidine</td><td>Norpethidine (neuroexcitatory)</td><td>Seizures, tremor, myoclonus, agitation; worse with repeated dosing or renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Oxycodone</td><td>Oxymorphone (active, more potent; via CYP2D6); noroxycodone (weakly active; via CYP3A4)</td><td>Parent and metabolites renally excreted &mdash; accumulate in renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Codeine</td><td>Morphine (via CYP2D6)</td><td>Marked interindividual variability &mdash; ultra-rapid metabolisers risk toxicity, poor metabolisers get little analgesia</td></tr><tr><td>Tramadol</td><td>O-desmethyltramadol (M1; active at the &mu;-receptor, via CYP2D6)</td><td>Contributes much of the opioid analgesia; accumulates in renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Diamorphine (heroin)</td><td>6-monoacetylmorphine, then morphine (&rarr; M6G / M3G)</td><td>Rapidly hydrolysed &mdash; effectively a morphine prodrug</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Opioids without clinically significant active metabolites</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Opioid</th><th>Metabolism and note</th></tr><tr><td>Fentanyl</td><td>Hepatic CYP3A4 &rarr; norfentanyl (inactive). Preferred in renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Alfentanil</td><td>Hepatic CYP3A4 &rarr; inactive metabolites. Preferred in renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Remifentanil</td><td>Non-specific plasma / tissue esterases &rarr; inactive acid metabolite; independent of hepatic and renal function</td></tr><tr><td>Methadone</td><td>Hepatic (CYP2B6 and CYP3A4; minor CYP2D6) &rarr; inactive EDDP. Favourable in renal impairment; watch QT and parent-drug accumulation</td></tr><tr><td>Hydromorphone</td><td>Hepatic glucuronidation &rarr; hydromorphone-3-glucuronide: not analgesic but neuroexcitatory &mdash; a partial exception that still warrants caution in renal impairment</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical relevance</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Morphine, pethidine and oxycodone have renally-cleared active or toxic metabolites &mdash; dose-reduce, extend the dosing interval, or avoid in renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Fentanyl and alfentanil are generally preferred when renal function is poor</td></tr><tr><td>Pethidine&rsquo;s norpethidine makes it unsuitable for repeated dosing or use in renal impairment, whatever the indication</td></tr><tr><td>Hydromorphone-3-glucuronide is a partial exception &mdash; analgesically inactive but neuroexcitatory, so caution still applies in renal impairment</td></tr></table></div></div></div></div>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_mtf_opioid_active_metabolites_001';

-- ===== Methadone CYP2B6/CYP3A4 wording: q_mtf_methadone_buprenorphine_comparison_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Methadone and buprenorphine differ fundamentally in receptor mechanism, cardiac safety profile, and how easily they are displaced by other opioids &mdash; distinctions that matter for both substitution therapy and acute pain management in patients established on either drug.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Methadone acts as a mu-opioid receptor agonist (not antagonist) alongside its NMDA receptor antagonist activity &mdash; this dual mechanism contributes to its efficacy in both opioid substitution therapy and neuropathic pain.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Buprenorphine has high affinity but low intrinsic activity (partial agonism) at the mu-opioid receptor &mdash; this combination means it binds tightly and displaces other opioids easily, while producing a submaximal effect, contributing to its ceiling effect and reduced respiratory depression risk.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> &mdash; It is buprenorphine, not methadone, that is associated with less QTc prolongation. Methadone causes dose-dependent QTc prolongation, with ECG monitoring recommended at initiation and particularly at doses above 120mg/day.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Because buprenorphine binds the mu-receptor with high affinity, displacing it requires high doses of full mu-agonists, not regular/standard doses &mdash; a clinically important consideration when managing acute pain or precipitated withdrawal in patients established on buprenorphine.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Metabolism of both methadone and buprenorphine is accelerated as pregnancy progresses, particularly in the third trimester, due to physiological and pharmacokinetic changes including increased CYP3A4 expression (and, for methadone, increased CYP2B6 activity) &mdash; both often require dose adjustment and more frequent dosing in pregnancy as a result.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Comparison of Methadone and Buprenorphine</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Methadone</strong> is a &mu;-opioid receptor agonist as well as an NMDA-receptor antagonist and monoamine reuptake inhibitor, metabolised in the liver to inactive metabolites by CYP2B6 and CYP3A4. Patients on methadone maintenance therapy should continue their usual oral dose perioperatively where possible, with additional immediate-release opioids for acute pain; if nil by mouth, rotation to an i.v. fentanyl or morphine infusion prevents withdrawal and provides analgesia.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Buprenorphine</strong> is a partial agonist at &mu;-receptors and an antagonist at &kappa; and &delta; receptors. Its high receptor affinity and slow dissociation give it an extended duration of action, used in addiction medicine to suppress withdrawal and craving for 24&#8211;48 hours. High-dose sublingual buprenorphine can theoretically antagonise a full agonist, risking either withdrawal or analgesic failure; current practice favours continuing the patient&rsquo;s usual dose while maximising opioid-sparing adjuncts and using full agonists (at appreciably higher doses than usual) for acute pain, sometimes in a high-dependency setting.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Naltrexone</strong> is a long-acting competitive opioid antagonist (duration ~48&#8211;72h) used to maintain abstinence. Chronic use doubles brain &mu;- and &delta;-opioid receptor density, so patients can become extremely opioid-sensitive once it is stopped; it is recommended to stop naltrexone 72h before surgery, maximising opioid-sparing strategies and considering a high-dependency setting postoperatively.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Methadone vs Buprenorphine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Methadone</th><th>Buprenorphine</th></tr><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Mu-opioid receptor agonist + NMDA receptor antagonist</td><td>Partial mu-opioid receptor agonist &mdash; high affinity, low intrinsic activity</td></tr><tr><td>Onset / elimination</td><td>Slow onset, long elimination half-life (22&#8211;24h); once-daily dosing usually prevents withdrawal</td><td>High receptor affinity displaces other opioids easily, even at low doses; high doses of full agonists needed to displace it</td></tr><tr><td>QTc effect</td><td>Dose-dependent QTc prolongation; ECG monitoring recommended at initiation and doses &gt;120mg/day</td><td>Less QTc prolongation</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolism in pregnancy</td><td>Accelerated as pregnancy progresses (increased CYP2B6 and CYP3A4 activity); dose adjustment/more frequent dosing often needed</td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4; also accelerated in pregnancy, especially third trimester; dose adjustment/more frequent dosing often needed</td></tr><tr><td>Analgesic vs withdrawal-suppression duration</td><td>Analgesic duration (4&#8211;8h) much shorter than withdrawal-suppression duration (24&#8211;48h); divided dosing (every 6&#8211;8h) may optimise peripartum analgesia</td><td>Divided dosing (3&#8211;4 times daily, every 6&#8211;8h) may similarly optimise peripartum analgesia</td></tr><tr><td>Peripartum analgesia interaction</td><td>No evidence that dose/interval affects neuraxial analgesia</td><td>Higher peri-delivery pain scores and post-Caesarean analgesic requirements with chronic use; full agonists with high intrinsic activity (sufentanil, fentanyl, hydromorphone, morphine) should be used for additional analgesia &mdash; sufentanil has higher receptor affinity than buprenorphine</td></tr><tr><td>Post-delivery management</td><td>Continue after delivery; split dosing may help</td><td>Continue after delivery; split dosing may help</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_mtf_methadone_buprenorphine_comparison_001';

-- ===== Methadone CYP2B6/CYP3A4 wording: q_sba_buprenorphine_peripartum_analgesia_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Methadone causes dose-dependent QTc prolongation via cardiac hERG potassium channel blockade, with ECG monitoring recommended at initiation and particularly above 120mg/day, whereas buprenorphine is associated with a comparatively lower risk of QTc prolongation.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. Correct.</strong> This accurately reflects the recognised cardiac safety profile for methadone.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> This describes methadone, not buprenorphine &mdash; methadone&rsquo;s analgesic duration (4&#8211;8 hours) is significantly shorter than its withdrawal-suppression duration (24&#8211;48 hours), which is why once-daily dosing suffices for withdrawal prevention but divided dosing is needed to maximise analgesia.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> It is buprenorphine that has the higher receptor affinity, making it harder to displace and requiring high doses of full agonists to overcome this in the acute pain setting.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Both methadone and buprenorphine should be continued after delivery, with split/divided dosing potentially beneficial in the peripartum period for both.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> The reverse is true; substances with high intrinsic activity at the mu-receptor (e.g. sufentanil, fentanyl, hydromorphone, morphine) should be used for peripartum analgesia in patients on buprenorphine, as these are more able to overcome buprenorphine’s high-affinity receptor occupancy and provide effective additional analgesia; sufentanil in particular has a higher binding affinity than buprenorphine itself.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Comparison of Methadone and Buprenorphine</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Methadone</strong> is a &mu;-opioid receptor agonist as well as an NMDA-receptor antagonist and monoamine reuptake inhibitor, metabolised in the liver to inactive metabolites by CYP2B6 and CYP3A4. Patients on methadone maintenance therapy should continue their usual oral dose perioperatively where possible, with additional immediate-release opioids for acute pain; if nil by mouth, rotation to an i.v. fentanyl or morphine infusion prevents withdrawal and provides analgesia.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Buprenorphine</strong> is a partial agonist at &mu;-receptors and an antagonist at &kappa; and &delta; receptors. Its high receptor affinity and slow dissociation give it an extended duration of action, used in addiction medicine to suppress withdrawal and craving for 24&#8211;48 hours. High-dose sublingual buprenorphine can theoretically antagonise a full agonist, risking either withdrawal or analgesic failure; current practice favours continuing the patient&rsquo;s usual dose while maximising opioid-sparing adjuncts and using full agonists (at appreciably higher doses than usual) for acute pain, sometimes in a high-dependency setting.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>Naltrexone</strong> is a long-acting competitive opioid antagonist (duration ~48&#8211;72h) used to maintain abstinence. Chronic use doubles brain &mu;- and &delta;-opioid receptor density, so patients can become extremely opioid-sensitive once it is stopped; it is recommended to stop naltrexone 72h before surgery, maximising opioid-sparing strategies and considering a high-dependency setting postoperatively.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Methadone vs Buprenorphine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Methadone</th><th>Buprenorphine</th></tr><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Mu-opioid receptor agonist + NMDA receptor antagonist</td><td>Partial mu-opioid receptor agonist &mdash; high affinity, low intrinsic activity</td></tr><tr><td>Onset / elimination</td><td>Slow onset, long elimination half-life (22&#8211;24h); once-daily dosing usually prevents withdrawal</td><td>High receptor affinity displaces other opioids easily, even at low doses; high doses of full agonists needed to displace it</td></tr><tr><td>QTc effect</td><td>Dose-dependent QTc prolongation; ECG monitoring recommended at initiation and doses &gt;120mg/day</td><td>Less QTc prolongation</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolism in pregnancy</td><td>Accelerated as pregnancy progresses (increased CYP2B6 and CYP3A4 activity); dose adjustment/more frequent dosing often needed</td><td>Hepatic, via CYP3A4; also accelerated in pregnancy, especially third trimester; dose adjustment/more frequent dosing often needed</td></tr><tr><td>Analgesic vs withdrawal-suppression duration</td><td>Analgesic duration (4&#8211;8h) much shorter than withdrawal-suppression duration (24&#8211;48h); divided dosing (every 6&#8211;8h) may optimise peripartum analgesia</td><td>Divided dosing (3&#8211;4 times daily, every 6&#8211;8h) may similarly optimise peripartum analgesia</td></tr><tr><td>Peripartum analgesia interaction</td><td>No evidence that dose/interval affects neuraxial analgesia</td><td>Higher peri-delivery pain scores and post-Caesarean analgesic requirements with chronic use; full agonists with high intrinsic activity (sufentanil, fentanyl, hydromorphone, morphine) should be used for additional analgesia &mdash; sufentanil has higher receptor affinity than buprenorphine</td></tr><tr><td>Post-delivery management</td><td>Continue after delivery; split dosing may help</td><td>Continue after delivery; split dosing may help</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_sba_buprenorphine_peripartum_analgesia_001';

-- ===== Methadone CYP2B6/CYP3A4 wording: q_sba_gabapentin_renal_excretion_half_life_001 =====
update questions
set data = data || '{"explanation":"<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">Gabapentin undergoes <strong>negligible hepatic metabolism</strong> and is excreted almost entirely <strong>unchanged by the kidney</strong>, with a plasma half-life of approximately <strong>5&ndash;7 hours</strong>. Baclofen is the key distractor: it is also renally excreted, but its half-life is shorter.</p><p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Baclofen is also excreted predominantly unchanged by the kidney, making it a plausible distractor, but its half-life is shorter &mdash; approximately <strong>2.5&ndash;4 hours</strong> (commonly cited as ~3 hours) &mdash; and does not match the 6-hour figure given.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> Gabapentin, a gabapentinoid used for neuropathic pain, undergoes <strong>negligible hepatic metabolism</strong> and is excreted almost entirely <strong>unchanged by the kidney</strong>. Its plasma half-life is approximately <strong>6 hours</strong>, necessitating dose reduction in renal impairment.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Amitriptyline is extensively <strong>hepatically metabolised</strong> (via CYP2D6/CYP1A2/CYP3A4) to nortriptyline and other metabolites, with a much <strong>longer</strong> half-life (10&ndash;28 hours).</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Methadone is extensively <strong>hepatically metabolised</strong> (via CYP2B6 and CYP3A4) to inactive metabolites, with a much <strong>longer and highly variable</strong> half-life (8&ndash;60+ hours).</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Diclofenac is an NSAID that is extensively <strong>hepatically metabolised</strong> (via CYP2C9), with a much <strong>shorter</strong> half-life (~1&ndash;2 hours).</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Gabapentinoids</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification and Mechanism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Structurally related to GABA, but <strong>no direct action at GABA receptors</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Both bind to the <strong>alpha-2-delta (&alpha;2&delta;) subunit of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels</strong>, reducing calcium influx and the subsequent release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, noradrenaline, substance P) from presynaptic nerve terminals</td></tr><tr><td>Net effect: reduced neuronal excitability &mdash; anti-epileptic, analgesic and anxiolytic effects</td></tr></table><div style=\"text-align:center;margin-top:12px\"><img src=\"images/gabapentinoid-a2d-mechanism.svg\" alt=\"Gabapentin and pregabalin bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx and release of glutamate, noradrenaline and substance P, with no direct action at GABA receptors\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:4px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Pharmacokinetics</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Gabapentin</th><th>Pregabalin</th></tr><tr><td>Hepatic metabolism</td><td>None</td><td>Negligible</td></tr><tr><td>Renal excretion</td><td>Almost entirely unchanged</td><td>Almost entirely unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Half-life</td><td>5&ndash;7 hours</td><td>5&ndash;7 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Absorption / bioavailability</td><td><strong>Non-linear</strong> &mdash; saturable absorption via an L-amino acid transporter, so bioavailability falls as the dose rises</td><td><strong>Linear</strong> &mdash; dose-proportional plasma concentration</td></tr><tr><td>Typical dosing</td><td>Three times daily</td><td>Twice daily</td></tr></table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Both depend on renal excretion, so doses must be reduced in renal impairment.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Efficacy and Harm (Gabapentin)</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td><strong>NNT</strong></td><td>&ge;50% pain reduction in chronic neuropathic pain (overall)</td><td>~6&ndash;8</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NNH</strong></td><td>Minor side effects</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NNH</strong></td><td>Major side effects</td><td>12</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Clinical Uses</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Licensed in the UK</th><td>Gabapentin and pregabalin are both licensed for neuropathic pain; gabapentin is also licensed for partial seizures and postherpetic neuralgia specifically</td></tr><tr><th>Neuropathic pain</th><td>Painful diabetic neuropathy, postherpetic neuralgia, and other peripheral/central neuropathic pain</td></tr><tr><th>Pregabalin only</th><td>Additionally licensed for <strong>generalised anxiety disorder</strong> and as an adjunct for focal seizures</td></tr><tr><th>Perioperative use</th><td>Gabapentinoids reduce postoperative pain and opioid requirements, and reduce the incidence of vomiting, pruritus and urinary retention &mdash; but <strong>increase the risk of sedation</strong></td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Common Side Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Side effect</th></tr><tr><td>Dizziness, somnolence/sedation, ataxia</td></tr><tr><td>Peripheral oedema, weight gain</td></tr><tr><td>Dry mouth, blurred vision</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Dependence and Misuse Potential</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Both <strong>reclassified as Class C, Schedule 3 controlled drugs</strong> in the UK (April 2019) because of recognised dependence and misuse potential</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal symptoms (insomnia, headache, nausea, anxiety, sweating) can occur on abrupt discontinuation &mdash; <strong>taper gradually</strong></td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb
where id = 'q_sba_gabapentin_renal_excretion_half_life_001';
